  "body": "### What is the issue with the URL Pattern Standard?\n\nRather than requiring engineers to bridge the gap themselves the following functions and properties would be appreciated\n\n```js\n// Having a class type makes calls to URLPattern.exec(...) return an instance\n// of something (e.g. URLPatternExecResults). Also allows results.constructor.name\n// to be something identifiable.\nclass URLPatternExecResults /* right now seems to be just an object */ {\n  ...\n  \n  // makes [object Object] syntax become [object URLPatternExecResults]\n  // in this example\n  get [Symbol.toStringTag]() { return this.constructor.name }\n\n  // allows iteration of a URLPattern.exec object results with the input,\n  // URL instance and the URLPattern.exec result in each iteration\n  *[Symbol.iterator]() {\n    for (const [index, urlString] of Object.entries(this.inputs)) {\n      yield [urlString, this.toURL(index), this]\n    }\n  }\n\n  // This should always work since URLPattern.exec(invalidURL) returns null\n  toString() {\n    return this.inputs?.[0]\n  }\n\n  get firstURL() {\n    // cache this if you'd like\n    return this.toURL(0)\n  }\n\n  // Grab, by default the first, input and wrap it in a call to new URL()\n  toURL(index = 0) {\n    try {\n      let url = new URL(this.inputs?.[index])\n      return url\n    }\n    catch (typeError) {\n      return null\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nMany folks will have to use the results from `URLPattern.exec()` as a URL to grab and handle URL parameters and other aspects using the standardized URL class. ",
